var s={isochrone:`<h2>Network isochrone</h2><p>_Created 2026-10-14 \xB7 Updated 2026-10-14_</p><p>Draws the area reachable within a stated walking time along a street network, from one or more sites.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>What it expects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sites</code></td><td>the points to walk from \u2014 pumps, clinics, depots</td></tr><tr><td><code>network</code></td><td>a street network, from <strong>Street network</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>It produces polygons, one per site, which a map can draw and <strong>Summarize points in polygons</strong> can count into.</p><h3>What you set</h3><ul><li><strong>Minutes</strong> and <strong>metres per minute</strong> \u2014 together these are the distance budget.</li></ul><p>5 minutes at 75 m/min is 375 m of street.</p><ul><li><strong>Corridor width</strong> \u2014 how wide a band to draw around the reachable streets.</li><li><strong>Snap limit</strong> \u2014 how far a site may be from the nearest junction before the</li></ul><p>run stops.</p><ul><li><strong>UTM zone and hemisphere</strong> \u2014 the projection it measures in. Every widget in a</li></ul><p>workflow that measures must agree on this one, and the run is refused by name if they disagree.</p><h3>What it assumes</h3><p><strong>A walk has three parts, and this charges for one of them.</strong> From where you stand to the nearest junction; along the streets; from the last junction to the door. Only the middle part is counted.</p><ul><li>The walk <strong>onto</strong> the network is measured \u2014 you can see it in the run evidence</li></ul><p>as <code>fw:networkSnaps</code> \u2014 and then not deducted from your budget. The catchment reaches slightly further than the minutes say. On the Soho example that is 10.9 m of a 375 m budget, about 3%.</p><ul><li>The walk <strong>off</strong> the network, from the last junction to a doorway, is not</li></ul><p>modelled. The smooth edge is a fixed corridor width, not a walk.</p><ul><li>A site <strong>further than the snap limit</strong> from any mapped street **stops the</li></ul><p>run**. It is not shown as hard to reach; it produces an error.</p><h3>Who this misleads, and in which direction</h3><p>All three push the same way: <strong>a poorly connected place looks better served than it is, or disappears from the result.</strong></p><p>Streets and footpaths are mapped unevenly, and informal settlements least of all. So the places that would show the worst access are the ones most likely to be excluded altogether. Read a catchment over sparsely mapped ground as optimistic, and check <code>fw:networkSnaps</code> for how far each site had to move to reach a street.</p><h3>What it is not</h3><p>Not a service area, not a catchment anyone agreed to, and not a routed travel time. The corridor is an approximation of shape, drawn to be read, not measured.</p><h3>Going deeper</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> measures the displacement on the example data. Experiment 114 records what would change it.</p>`,snap_to_network:`<h2>Snap to network</h2><p>_Created 2026-10-14 \xB7 Updated 2026-10-14_</p><p>Moves each site to the nearest junction of a street network, so that steps which route along streets have somewhere to start.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>What it expects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sites</code></td><td>the points to move</td></tr><tr><td><code>network</code></td><td>a street network, from <strong>Street network</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h3>What it assumes</h3><p><strong>It moves your data.</strong> Every site comes out at a junction, not where it stood. How far each one travelled is measured and published as <code>fw:greatestSnapDistanceMetres</code> in the run evidence, and the output points are a <strong>derived</strong> dataset \u2014 they are not observations any more.</p><p><strong>A site too far from the network stops the run.</strong> Past the snap limit it raises an error rather than being carried through as unroutable. A place with no mapped street nearby is therefore absent from the result rather than shown as badly served, and street mapping is uneven in a known direction.</p><h3>What to check</h3><p>The snap distance, every time. If a site moved 200 m to reach a junction, then everything measured downstream starts 200 m from where it should, and that error does not appear anywhere in the final numbers.</p><h3>Going deeper</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=19-snap-vs-pyproj.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">snapping notebook</a> checks the junction choice against pyproj and measures each displacement against a five-minute budget \u2014 one of the eight example pumps was decided by 0.43 m.</p>`,summarize_hull:`<h2>Observed extent (convex hull)</h2><p>_Created 2026-10-14 \xB7 Updated 2026-10-14_</p><p>Draws the smallest convex outline containing your points, and measures the area it encloses.</p><h3>What you connect</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>What it expects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>points</code></td><td>at least three located points</td></tr></tbody></table><p>It produces one polygon carrying <code>areaSquareMetres</code> and <code>spanMetres</code>, which a rule or a table can read.</p><h3>What it assumes</h3><p><strong>A hull is convex and your data may not be.</strong> Cases along a river, facilities along a coast \u2014 the hull fills in the space between them, so it <strong>asserts area where nothing was observed</strong>.</p><p><strong>It is an outline of what was seen, not a boundary.</strong> Not a service area, not a catchment, not anything anyone agreed to. Where observation stopped is not where the thing being observed stopped.</p><p><strong>Points without coordinates take no part</strong>, and the count that did is stated in the notes.</p><h3>What to check</h3><p>The span against the area. A hull far longer than it is wide is close to a line, and should be read as one \u2014 the widget says so in its notes when it happens.</p><h3>Going deeper</h3><p>The lab's <a href="https://digital-twin-ph.github.io/validation-lab/lab/index.html?path=10-hull-vs-shapely.ipynb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hull notebook</a> checks the outline and the area against Shapely.</p>`};var i=s,l=t=>Object.hasOwn(i,t),p=t=>`https://github.com/digital-twin-ph/fieldwork/blob/main/docs/widgets/${t}.md`;function g(t,a){return l(t)?`<button class="text-button" id="open-manual" data-manual="${a(t)}">Manual for this widget</button>`:""}function m(t,a,o){let n=i[t];if(!n)return;let e=document.createElement("dialog");e.id="manual-dialog",e.setAttribute("aria-labelledby","manual-title"),e.innerHTML=`<div class="manual-head"><h2 id="manual-title">${o(a)}</h2><button class="button" id="manual-close">Close</button></div><article class="manual-body">${n}</article><p class="muted">This page is part of the manual carried in this build. Its source is <a href="${p(t)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/widgets/${o(t)}.md</a>, and the widget's ontology entry points at it with <code>fw:userManual</code>.</p>`,document.body.append(e);let r=()=>{e.close(),e.remove()};e.querySelector("#manual-close").onclick=r,e.addEventListener("cancel",h=>{h.preventDefault(),r()}),e.showModal()}export{i as a,l as b,p as c,g as d,m as e};
